\value{
PAM returns an \code{mfrm_clusters} object containing \code{membership} (ID, Cluster,
Medoid, Silhouette), \code{cluster_summary}, numeric and categorical \code{profiles},
\code{medoids}, the reviewed \code{feature_data}, and \code{settings}. Silhouette values
describe within-sample separation, not membership probabilities or
resampling stability. \code{summary()} returns the cluster-size/silhouette table.
Numeric profiles report the mean, median and sample SD in the original
feature units. SD is \code{NA} for a one-entity group and zero for identical
values in a group with at least two entities. A singleton's silhouette is
zero by convention; it is not evidence of group separation.
Hierarchical and k-means results retain their respective subclasses and
method-specific tree/center components; see their linked help.

An \code{mfrm_cluster_comparison} object containing:
\itemize{
\item \code{analysis_summary}: method, distance, fitted space, scaling, retained
component count, linkage (unavailable for PAM and k-means), group
count, selected feature count (\code{Features}), included/excluded entity counts,
smallest/largest group sizes, and mean silhouette for each analysis and
imputation. \code{Imputation} is \code{NA} for ordinary clustering results.
\item \code{weights}: selected features and their supplied weights for each
analysis. Unselected features have no row; they are not zero-weight inputs.
\item \code{comparisons}: all pairs of analyses, with one row per imputation.
\code{Pairs} counts unordered pairs of included entities, excluding self-pairs.
\code{SplitPairs} were together in \code{First} and apart in \code{Second}; \code{JoinedPairs}
were apart in \code{First} and together in \code{Second}. \code{ChangedFraction} is
their sum divided by \code{Pairs}. \code{AdjustedRand} is the adjusted Rand index.
\item \code{comparison_summary}: number of compared partitions, mean/minimum/
maximum changed fraction, and mean adjusted Rand index for each analysis
pair. \code{summary()} returns this table.
\item \code{analyses}: the original results, including omitted IDs and all
imputation-specific partitions, profiles, and settings.
}

An \code{mfrm_imputed_clusters} object containing \code{analyses} (one
\code{mfrm_clusters} object per imputation), \code{co_membership} (a symmetric matrix
indexed by ID), \code{analysis_summary}, original \code{feature_data}, \code{imputed_cells}
with original reasons, the full \code{imputation_model}, and \code{settings}.
Every available matrix entry uses all \code{settings$imputations} analyses as
its denominator; pairs involving excluded entities are \code{NA}, including
their diagonal entries. \code{summary()} returns per-imputation counts and mean
silhouette widths. Numeric ranges are retained in each analysis.
Hierarchical partitions inherit from \code{mfrm_clusters} and retain their
trees. Method and linkage are retained in \code{settings}.

Invisibly, a \code{ggplot} object. \code{\link[=plot_data]{plot_data()}} extracts the plotted
table or matrix, title, subtitle, legend, and excluded IDs. Heatmaps also
retain the displayed IDs and the number of imputations. Profile plots also retain
\code{observations}: original-unit entity values and group/exclusion status,
suitable for custom distribution plots; see \code{\link[=plot_data]{plot_data()}}.
\code{\link[=as_ggplot]{as_ggplot()}} converts imputation co-membership heatmaps using the saved
matrix, ID order, label choice, colours and imputation count. The default
and \code{component = "matrix"} retain the complete view and its fixed
zero-to-one scale. Unavailable cells have both grey fill and crosses,
distinguishing them from zero even in monochrome. Metadata, including
excluded IDs, remain available with \code{\link[=plot_data]{plot_data()}}. No values are
recomputed or renormalized when IDs are selected. Use
\code{ggplot2::labs(title = NULL, subtitle = NULL, caption = NULL)} to hide
annotations while retaining the source data.
Silhouette conversion retains negative widths, saved order and the overall
mean reference, with group labels independent of colour. Numeric profiles
retain original-unit means/medians and counts; circles and triangles are
offset vertically to show coincident values without adding intervals.
Categorical profiles retain the original category order, unused levels,
group counts and a fixed zero-to-one proportion scale. Default and
\code{component = "table"} preserve the complete selected view; categorical
profiles also accept \code{component = "matrix"}. These conversions do not
recluster, select groups, or estimate uncertainty.
}
\description{
Group an entity-level feature table using PAM, hierarchical clustering
or numeric k-means. Gower-based methods use the optional \code{cluster} package.
Use \code{mfrm_cluster()} and select \code{method = "pam"} (the default),
\code{"hierarchical"}, or \code{"kmeans"}. Each method retains its own geometry,
settings and result class; the method is never selected from the data.

Compare existing external-feature clustering results without refitting.
Review how group membership changes when the selected features, number of
groups, feature weights, or clustering method changes, including paired comparisons across
the same imputations.

The former \code{mfrm_cluster_pam()} entry is no longer exported;
use \code{mfrm_cluster(..., method = "pam")}. Its calculation is unchanged.

Apply the same external-feature clustering analysis to each completed data set from \code{mice},
retaining all analyses and the fraction of imputations in which each pair
of entities belongs to the same group.

Inspect within-sample separation, individual feature profiles, or pairwise
co-membership across imputations using existing clustering results.
}
\details{
\strong{PAM Gower clustering (\code{method = "pam"}, default)}: Numeric differences are
divided by included-entity ranges, nominal features use match/mismatch,
logical features use symmetric binary differences, and ordered factors use
equally spaced declared-level ranks divided by observed rank range; feature
contributions are combined by \code{weights / sum(weights)} in \verb{[0, 1]}. Every
selected feature must vary, and included entities are capped at 5,000
(quadratic pairwise memory). PAM uses deterministic BUILD/SWAP
initialization (\code{\link[cluster:daisy]{cluster::daisy()}}, \code{\link[cluster:pam]{cluster::pam()}}).

\strong{Silhouette and scope}: For entity \eqn{i}, \eqn{s(i) = (b(i) - a(i)) /
\max(a(i), b(i)) \in [-1, 1]} (\code{\link[cluster:silhouette]{cluster::silhouette()}}, Rousseeuw, 1987,
\doi{10.1016/0377-0427(87)90125-7}); singletons and zero denominators receive
\code{0} by convention, and excluded IDs receive \code{NA}. Partitions are exploratory
descriptions of external attributes, without latent-ability or rater-quality
inference; see the \href{https://ryuya-dot-com.github.io/mfrmr/dev/articles/mfrmr-external-features.html}{online feature-analysis guide}.

Comparisons align entities by ID: \code{ChangedFraction = 0} and
\code{AdjustedRand = 1} indicate identical partitions up to group relabeling.
Silhouettes computed under different feature sets, scalings, or weights use
different geometries and are not automatic selection criteria. Across
multiple imputations, summary means and ranges describe sensitivity across
the retained completions rather than Rubin-pooled inference; conflicting
shared feature values or differing inclusion masks fail closed.

Fit and diagnose the imputation model in \code{\link[mice:mice]{mice::mice()}} first (excluding the
ID column from imputation/predictors and declaring eligible cells via
\code{where} and \code{impute}). Observed values, types, and factor levels must match
\code{x} by ID; any invalid completion or failed clustering stops with its
imputation index. Gower ranges, k-means standardization, and per-completion
PCA bases are recomputed within each completed sample (capped at 5,000
entities). Pairwise \code{co_membership} proportions are label-invariant
sensitivity summaries across the supplied completions, not Rubin-pooled
estimates or posterior membership probabilities.

\strong{Hierarchical Gower clustering (\code{method = "hierarchical"})}: Uses
\code{\link[stats:hclust]{stats::hclust()}} and \code{\link[stats:cutree]{stats::cutree()}} at \code{k} with \code{linkage = "average"}
(default, UPGMA) or \code{"complete"} (Ward linkage is unsupported for mixed
Gower dissimilarities; Murtagh & Legendre, 2014,
\doi{10.1007/s00357-014-9161-z}). Returns an \code{mfrm_hierarchical_clusters}
subclass retaining \code{tree} (\code{medoids} is \code{NULL}). Tied merge heights use
merge order at cut \code{k}. Default \code{plot()} draws the stored dendrogram with
\code{k}-group boxes (\code{component = "tree"} in \code{\link[=plot_data]{plot_data()}}); \code{"silhouette"} and
\code{"profile"} views match \code{plot.mfrm_clusters()}.

\strong{Numeric k-means (\code{method = "kmeans"})}: Uses \code{\link[stats:kmeans]{stats::kmeans()}} with
Hartigan-Wong updates (\code{nstart = 25}, \code{iter.max = 100}, \code{seed = 1},
\code{scale = TRUE}, \code{silhouette = TRUE} up to 5,000 included entities). Numeric
preprocessing matches \code{\link[=mfrm_pca]{mfrm_pca()}}; when \code{x} is an \code{mfrm_pca} result, its
saved scores are used without respecifying \code{weights}, \code{scale}, or \code{missing}.
Warnings, nonconvergence, and nonfinite sums of squares fail closed. Returns
an \code{mfrm_clusters} object with \code{centers}, \code{withinss}, \code{totss}, and
\code{tot.withinss} (\code{medoids} is \code{NULL}); \code{1 - tot.withinss / totss} is
descriptive fitted-space scatter, not predictive accuracy or a test of \code{k}.

No model or clustering is refitted. Silhouette widths describe separation
in the fitted sample, not stability or probabilities. The dashed line is
the overall mean silhouette; excluded entities have no silhouette.

Feature profiles describe one partition; numeric summaries have no confidence
intervals. For an imputed result, inspect a completion with
\code{plot(x$analyses[[1]], type = "profile", feature = "ExperienceYears")}.
Cluster numbers must not be averaged across imputations.

Imputation heatmaps show the fraction of all supplied imputations in which
each pair belongs to the same group, on a fixed zero-to-one scale. Grey cells
are unavailable pairs involving excluded entities, not zero co-membership.
These fractions describe sensitivity to imputations under fixed settings,
not membership probabilities, sampling stability, or a consensus partition.
Rows and columns follow input order or explicit \code{ids}; no hierarchical
clustering is performed. PAM is nonhierarchical and these plots do not
provide a dendrogram. Use \code{mfrm_cluster(..., method = "hierarchical")} for a separate
hierarchical analysis and its dendrogram.
}
